As shown in the figure, let \( S-ABCD \) be a quadrilateral with a height of 3, and the base \( ABCD \) is a square with side length of 2. The projection of the vertex \( S \) on the base \( ABCD \) is the center of the square \( ABCD \). Point \( K \) is the midpoint of edge \( SC \). A plane passing through \( AK \) intersects segments \( SB \) and \( SD \) at points \( M \) and \( N \), respectively ( \(M \) and \( N \) can be endpoints of the segments).

(1) Find the sine value of the angle between line \( AK \) and plane \( SBC \);
(2) Prove that \( \frac{V_{S-MKN}}{V_{S-BCD}} = \frac{SM \cdot SK \cdot SN}{SB \cdot SC \cdot SD} \), and calculate the volume of quadrilateral \( S-AMKN \) when \( M \) is the midpoint of \( SB \).

s:
1. **Identifying Relationships Between Angles:**
   \[
   \forall \text{ angles: }  
   \begin{cases} 
   \widehat{D^{\prime} A B} &= \widehat{D A D^{\prime}} \\
   \widehat{B^{\prime} B A} &= \widehat{B^{\prime} B C} \\
   \widehat{B^{\prime} C B} &= \widehat{B^{\prime} C D} \\
   \widehat{C D D^{\prime}} &= \widehat{D^{\prime} D A} 
   \end{cases}
   \]

2. **Using Triangle Angle Sum Property:**
   \[
   \begin{aligned}
   \text{In triangle } \triangle D^{\prime} A B \text{ (angle sum property gives):} \\
   \widehat{C^{\prime} D^{\prime} A^{\prime}} &= 180^{\circ} - w - z \quad \text{(where $w = \angle D^{\prime} A B$ and $z = \angle D A D^{\prime}$)} \\
   \text{In triangle } \triangle B^{\prime} B C \text{ (angle sum property gives):} \\
   \widehat{C^{\prime} B^{\prime} A^{\prime}} &= 180^{\circ} - x - y \quad \text{(where $x = \angle B^{\prime} B A$ and $y = \angle B^{\prime} B C$)}
   \end{aligned}
   \]

3. **Summing All Angles:**
   \[
   2w + 2x + 2y + 2z = 360^{\circ} \Rightarrow w + x + y + z = 180^{\circ}
   \]

4. **Verifying the Cocyclic Nature:**
   \[
   \widehat{C^{\prime} B^{\prime} A^{\prime}} + \widehat{C^{\prime} D^{\prime} A^{\prime}} = (180^{\circ} - x - y) + (180^{\circ} - w - z)
   \]

5. **Simplifying the Sum:**
   \[
   \begin{aligned}
   \widehat{C^{\prime} B^{\prime} A^{\prime}} + \widehat{C^{\prime} D^{\prime} A^{\prime}} &= (180^{\circ} - x - y) + (180^{\circ} - w - z) \\
   &= 180^{\circ} - (w+x+y+z) \\
   &= 180^{\circ}
   \end{aligned}
   \]

6. **Conclusion:**
   Given that the sum of the opposite angles $\widehat{C^{\prime} B^{\prime} A^{\prime}}$ and $\widehat{C^{\prime} D^{\prime} A^{\prime}}$ is 180°, the points \(A^{\prime}, B^{\prime}, C^{\prime},\) and \(D^{\prime}\) are cocyclic. This concludes the proof that these points lie on the same circle.

\[
\boxed{}
